Here's how to handle draft persistence with .NET MAUI's lifecycle events:

**In your `NotePage.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    private const string DraftKey = "NoteDraft";

    public NotePage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // Subscribe to lifecycle events
        App.Current.Windows[0].Deactivated += OnAppBackgrounded;
        App.Current.Windows[0].Activated += OnAppResumed;
    }

    private void OnAppBackgrounded(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // Save draft to Preferences (lightweight key-value store)
        Preferences.Set(DraftKey, NoteEditor.Text ?? string.Empty);
    }

    private void OnAppResumed(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // Restore only if editor is empty (avoid overwriting active edits)
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(NoteEditor.Text))
        {
            NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
        }
    }

    // Clean up when navigating away
    protected override void OnDisappearing()
    {
        base.OnDisappearing();
        App.Current.Windows[0].Deactivated -= OnAppBackgrounded;
        App.Current.Windows[0].Activated -= OnAppResumed;
    }

    // Clear draft after explicit save
    private void OnSaveClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // ... your save logic ...
        Preferences.Remove(DraftKey);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`Window.Deactivated`** fires when the app loses focus (backgrounded).
- **`Window.Activated`** fires when the app regains focus.
- **`Preferences`** is ideal for small text. For large drafts, use `SecureStorage` or write to `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory`.
- Always unsubscribe from events in `OnDisappearing` to avoid leaks.
- Clear the draft after the user explicitly saves.

**Alternative approach using `App.xaml.cs`** (centralised):

```csharp
// In App.xaml.cs
protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState activationState)
{
    var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

    window.Deactivated += (s, e) =>
        WeakReferenceMessenger.Default.Send(new AppBackgroundedMessage());

    window.Activated += (s, e) =>
        WeakReferenceMessenger.Default.Send(new AppResumedMessage());

    return window;
}
```

This decouples lifecycle from your page using the MVVM Community Toolkit messenger pattern.
